Ok so I just spent 1 hour trying to figure out why my code wasn't working. I was trying to use the code below, but it just didn't seem to want to work. 

```
options.transform: function (body, response, resolveWithFullResponse) {
    return body.split('').reverse().join('');
};
```

The `body` was already transformed as JSON and I could not for the life of me understand why. _Why why why_ is there a transform function running before **my** transform function, I thought. Well, apparently `options.json` was set to `true` earlier in my code (because my requests were being sent to a json api) and this led to my transform function being rendered completely useless. 
### Workaround

Set `options.json` to `false` and then manually stringify the body before it's sent.
### Suggested solution

Disable the parsing if there is a transform function present. Or just run `options.transform` before parsing the body. _Or_ if this is intended behaviour, please make it more clear that this is going to happen if I switch the `options.json` flag.

